      ":question/id": "210b4c1c-1d03-5034-a3d7-a201ae48f53a",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(xy^{3}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(xy^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(xy^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=k+3\\cdot 4=k+12.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(xy^{3}\\right)=k+12.\n$$",
